WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:04.280
Film Courage: I think you’ve said previously
Shana that you love to write messy characters?

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:05.280
Why?

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:08.010
Shana Feste, writer/director of BOUNDARIES:
Yeah.

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:13.820
Well, I think I’m a messy person and I grew
up with one of the messiest characters ever

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:17.030
as a father who was just so complicated.

00:00:17.030 --> 00:00:21.040
And you know on paper you would look at my
father and think “Oh, my gosh.

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:22.040
Stay away.

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:24.530
Like you got the short stick in the father
department.”

00:00:24.530 --> 00:00:31.940
But when you met him and you saw how warm
and charismatic and caring he was, you might

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:35.559
start to change your perspective of him.

00:00:35.559 --> 00:00:37.540
So I think those are the most fascinating
characters.

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:41.390
We’re all over the place as human beings
and those are the characters I like writing.

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:43.329
Film Courage: What about playing them?

00:00:43.329 --> 00:00:46.659
Did you feel that your characters in the film
were messy?

00:00:46.659 --> 00:00:47.659
I know Henry [played by Lewis MacDougall in
Shana Feste’s film BOUNDARIES] was a good

00:00:47.659 --> 00:00:50.719
artist, he sure did draw within the lines.

00:00:50.719 --> 00:00:56.149
Lewis MacDougall, actor: Henry may be messy,
but you know I don’t think he really cared

00:00:56.149 --> 00:00:57.399
and that’s the great thing.

00:00:57.399 --> 00:01:01.210
And like you said, he does these amazing drawings,
these very explicit drawings.

00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:03.649
You know, he’s a very unique guy.

00:01:03.649 --> 00:01:06.700
I enjoyed playing him a lot.

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:14.430
Vera Farmiga, actor: I love messy characterizations
and my character would be the first to admit

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:17.310
that her life was a mess.

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:22.530
And that honesty, that is root to me, is enchanting.

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:27.340
And I love to fix messes and like I’m OCD
about cleaning up messes.

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:29.598
Film Courage: Well, I thought Laura [Laura
Jaconi, the protagonist played by Vera Farmiga

00:01:29.598 --> 00:01:33.000
in BOUNDARIES] was very good at keeping things
together despite the circumstances.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:34.000
Vera: Barely!

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.570
Film Courage: She seemed to be getting things
back together and then it would fall apart

00:01:38.570 --> 00:01:39.570
again?

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:40.570
Vera: She was striving to at least.

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:41.921
Film Courage: Well, it was enjoyable to watch.

00:01:41.921 --> 00:01:46.909
Shana, I know you received your MFA from UT
Austin [master’s degree in creative writing

00:01:46.909 --> 00:01:47.909
and screenwriting]?

00:01:47.909 --> 00:01:48.909
Shana: Yes.

00:01:48.909 --> 00:01:50.300
Film Courage: How has your writing changed
since those college days?

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:51.710
Shana: Well, I was a horrible writer.

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:53.679
I mean really, truly horrible.

00:01:53.679 --> 00:01:59.310
Like unreadable things I wrote back then and
I think that’s one great thing about writing

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:01.460
is the more you do it, the better you get.

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:07.009
So I teach occasionally and that’s the advice
I give to all my screenwriting students, just

00:02:07.009 --> 00:02:10.369
keep writing because you will get better.

00:02:10.369 --> 00:02:15.120
And I’m looking forward to in 30 years when
I write the most beautiful, epic screenplay

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:17.030
ever because I’m going to be that much better
then.

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:19.120
Film Courage: What about acting?

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:26.140
How do you feel that you’ve evolved from
last year or when you first started?

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:29.660
Vera: I don’t think about that at all, I
don’t.

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:35.400
I try to evolve as a human being and I try
to attain as many skills as I can.

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:42.819
I try to work on myself and understanding
my own foibles and transgressions and ineptitude

00:02:42.819 --> 00:02:47.769
and you try to self-diagnose often so that
you are good at diagnosing your characters.

00:02:47.769 --> 00:02:54.170
But I don’t know if I think about if I improved
in acting?

00:02:54.170 --> 00:03:00.860
I think I’m only as good as the writing
and in my scene partners.

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:06.560
Lewis: I think I’ve certainly learned a
lot from getting to work on this film and

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:08.140
getting to work with the amazing cast.

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:13.251
And like Vera said it’s your screen partners
and getting to work with Vera and Chris [Christopher

00:03:13.251 --> 00:03:15.560
Plummer plays the father Jack Jaconi in BOUNDARIES]
and everybody you know absolute legends.

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:19.400
They force you to bring your game up to match
their level.

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:24.189
Vera: And I think the only thing you are thinking
about as an actor is what’s my objective?

00:03:24.189 --> 00:03:26.221
What am I supposed to convey?

00:03:26.221 --> 00:03:29.599
What’s important to fulfill in this characterization?

00:03:29.599 --> 00:03:34.280
I don’t think you think about am I being
a good actor or am I doing a good job?

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:40.930
I think you’ll be doing a good job if you’ve
figured out what the obstacles are and what

00:03:40.930 --> 00:03:45.820
your objectives are and what are the character
traits that you were trying to convey.

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:48.249
I suppose that’s the way we think about
it.

